    <text>
        <body>
            <div>
                <p>The Marianne Moore Digital Archive (MMDA)
                    Notebooks Project makes digital
                    reproductions and transcriptions of
                    Marianne Moore’s notebooks readily
                    accessible to scholarly, classroom, and
                    non-academic readers for the first time.
                    The transcriptions are supported by
                    annotations contextualizing Moore’s
                    writing and life and providing citations
                    to the original source texts she invokes
                    or from which she is taking
                    notes—sometimes extensively. These
                    digital editions are also supported by a
                    growing collection of related materials,
                    such as a bibliography of Moore
                    criticism, an index of books Moore owned
                    (complete with inscriptions), a
                    glossary, an interactive timeline of
                    Moore's life and publications, links to
                    hard-to-find critical essays and to some
                    of the full source texts from which
                    Moore drew, and searchable reproductions
                    of the richly documented Marianne Moore
                    Newsletters. The site also provides
                    notebook-internal and inter-notebook
                    search tools. This site makes it
                    possible to understand Moore and her
                    work in a new way, revolutionizing Moore
                    criticism; contributing to popular and
                    scholarly understanding of the modernist
                    period; and developing new tools for the
                    digital editing and publication of
                    handwritten materials. Its tools for
                    research and for the sharing of
                    resources are constantly expanding and
                    under continuous development, as more
                    notebooks go online and more resources
                    become available to the MMDA team.</p>
                <p>Moore’s notebooks offer extraordinary
                    challenges for editors and digital
                    designers because they include multiple
                    genres, images (Moore sketched objects
                    that interested her), genetic layers of
                    text, and references to decades’ worth
                    of popular and academic source
                    materials. Especially her
                    poetry-drafting notebooks provide
                    evidence of her ongoing editing of
                    earlier thoughts and phrasing. These
                    fully edited and annotated notebooks
                    suggest the deep cultural genesis of her
                    poems: they constitute not nearly
                    finished drafts of poems and essays
                    (that is, pre-publication texts) but,
                    instead, a rich and varied collection of
                    notes from her reading, from overheard
                    conversations, and from her observations
                    at lectures, museums, concerts, and
                    sporting events from which Moore drew
                    the materials that went into her drafts
                    and published work. Part commonplace
                    book, part sketchbook, part reading
                    diary, part travelogue, each of Moore’s
                    notebooks offers an extraordinary window
                    into the eclectic print and visual
                    culture of the twentieth century and the
                    ways in which one of America’s most
                    innovative poets responded to her
                    world.</p>
                <p>The MMDA Notebooks Project addresses the
                    special challenges and opportunities
                    presented by these documents through
                    diplomatic transcriptions and brief
                    bibliographic and historical annotation.
                    Moreover, each notebook is accompanied
                    by an editor's introduction that
                    discusses the special textual or
                    bibliographic features of that
                    particular notebook as well as aspects
                    of its historical, literary, and
                    cultural significance. Cumulatively,
                    these notebooks make available an
                    unparalleled record of a major modernist
                    figure’s reading, thinking, citation,
                    and composition processes.</p>
                <p>The Notebooks Project also employs new
                    intelligent tools, developed for this
                    project, with which researchers in the
                    humanities can interact with handwritten
                    historical documents online. The MMDA
                    uses a customized version of EVT version
                    1.0 to publish digital editions of the
                    Notebooks, as well as the base for a
                    suite of in-browser manuscript editing
                    tools being developed at the University
                    at Buffalo to address the needs of the
                    site's editorial team and its users.
                    When completed, the MMDA hopes to make
                    this integrated, open source platform
                    for the production and publication of
                    transcribed and annotated manuscript
                    material available to other users of the
                    EVT and the digital scholarly editing
                    community. Through the collaboration of
                    scholars in English, Computer Science
                    and Engineering, and the Center for
                    Unified Biometrics and Sensors (CUBS) at
                    the University at Buffalo, we aim to
                    create a complete editorial workflow
                    solution for transcription and
                    annotation of manuscript collections. </p>
                <p>The <ref target="about/documentation.xml">Project Documentation</ref> page
                    posts the MMDA editorial transcription
                    guidelines and will soon publish our
                    encoding schema.</p>
            </div>
